<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- The GitHub Actions runner pool: one per host, like the cockpit beside it. This is host state,
     not repository state, which is why it lives outside templates/ and is installed by hand with
     __PLACEHOLDER__ substitution rather than rendered by bin/sync.mjs. The placeholders and the
     sed line that fills them are in the kit README, "The runner pool"; they are deliberately not
     named here, because a renderer substitutes them wherever they appear, comments included, and
     an instruction that names them turns into nonsense the moment it is rendered.

     Deliberately NOT `bash -lc`: a login shell sources ~/.profile, and anything exported there
     would reach a container that runs arbitrary pull-request code. The explicit PATH below is the
     whole environment this service gets, and it carries no credential at all - the registration
     organization token is read only by pool.sh; containers receive a short-lived registration token. A path is not a
     token; the same reasoning put GH_CONFIG_DIR rather than a credential into
     dev.cezar.cockpit.plist.

     KeepAlive is what makes the ordering problem disappear. This agent and OrbStack both start at
     login with no guarantee of sequence, so pool.sh is written to tolerate an unreachable Docker
     daemon and simply retry; if it dies for any other reason launchd restarts it. There is
     nothing to schedule and nothing to order.

     A LaunchAgent runs only while the user is logged in, and this service now gates production
     deploys - so the obvious hardening is /Library/LaunchDaemons. It was investigated and
     rejected: OrbStack's engine is itself a user application (it runs out of /Applications as the
     logged-in user, its socket is ~/.orbstack/run/docker.sock, and the only thing it puts in
     /Library/LaunchDaemons is an on-demand privhelper), so a pool daemon in the system domain
     would start with no engine and spin on `docker info` until somebody logged in. FileVault
     settles the cold-boot case anyway: nothing runs before a human unlocks the disk, which is the
     point of having it on a host where CI secrets now land. `sudo fdesetup authrestart` covers a
     planned restart; the RUNNER_CI / RUNNER_AGENT organisation variables cover everything else,
     by moving every job back to ubuntu-latest without touching a repository. The kit README,
     "Why this stays a LaunchAgent", has the evidence. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>dev.gha-runner.pool</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>__POOL_SH__</string>
  </array>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- Must contain the OrbStack docker shim, normally /usr/local/bin. -->
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>__PATH__</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>__HOME__</string>
    <key>GHA_RUNNER_ORG</key>
    <string>design4pro</string>
    <key>GHA_RUNNER_GROUP</key>
    <string>local-mac</string>
    <key>GHA_RUNNER_TOKEN_FILE</key>
    <string>__HOME__/.config/gha-runner/token</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/gha-runner-pool.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/gha-runner-pool.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
